p": -0.003957522567361593, "top": [[",", -0.003957522567361593], [" ML", -6.823609828948975]]}, {"t": " etc", "lp": -1.0277130603790283, "top": [[" etc", -1.0277130603790283], [" mixed", -1.0967381000518799]]}, {"t": ".)", "lp": -2.1457903130794875e-05, "top": [[".)", -2.1457903130794875e-05], [".", -11.798035621643066]]}, {"t": "", "lp": -0.5272178649902344, "top": [["", -0.5272178649902344], ["\n\n", -0.8927745819091797]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are handled by the Xe driver. Here's what each mode does:\n\n## CCS Mode 1\n- **Context stealing**: The driver will \"steal\" a CCS from another GPU context when one is needed but none is available\n- More flexible but can introduce **contention** between contexts competing for CCS resources\n- Useful when you want maximum flexibility in context scheduling\n\n## CCS Mode 2\n- **No stealing**: The driver will not steal CCS from other contexts\n- If no CCS is available, the operation may fall back or wait\n- More **predictable** behavior with less contention\n- Better for workloads where you want to avoid interference between contexts\n\n## CCS Mode 4\n- Typically relates to **explicit CCS management** or a specific scheduling policy\n- Often used for **deterministic** behavior in multi-GPU or specialized compute scenarios\n\n---\n\n**Important caveat:** I'm not fully certain about the exact semantics of mode 4, and these driver parameters can vary between driver versions and kernel releases. The Intel Xe driver documentation and kernel source are the authoritative sources here.\n\n**Where would you use mode 4?**\n- Likely in scenarios requiring **explicit control** over CCS allocation\n- Possibly for **debugging** or **performance tuning** in specialized compute workloads\n\n---\n\nWould you like me to help you find the authoritative documentation for your specific driver version? If so, it would help to know:\n- Your kernel version\n- Whether you're using the out-of-tree Intel GPU driver or the in-tree version\n- Your specific use case (gaming, compute, etc.)"}